<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
=== Writing a command ===
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
* '''Add from a template''' (first button) creates a ready-made command: copy the 3MF, open the project folder, web request, Shelly plug (Gen 1 or Gen 2, with or without a password, or through the Shelly cloud with an API key), Home Assistant, Jeedom, Klipper preheating, Python script. The part to replace, such as <code><nowiki><PLUG_IP></nowiki></code>, is selected: just type. Requests made with <code>curl</code> use <code>--fail</code>, so that an error answered by the device is reported as an error.
* '''Add from scratch''' adds an empty row.
* '''Move up''' / '''Move down''' change the order of the commands. "Before the export" commands are always run before the others.
* Under the table, the command of the selected row can be edited with:
** '''Browse…''': inserts the path of a program or file;
** '''Insert a variable''': inserts <code>%EXPORT%.3mf</code>, <code>%PROJECTDIR%</code>… (see below), with the quotes and the space when needed. The menu shows the real value of each variable for the current export.
* A '''check''', updated as you type, shows:
** whether the program is found, and where;
** how the command is split into parameters, with the real paths of the current export;
** the usual mistakes: command built into Windows such as <code>copy</code> without <code>cmd /c</code>, part of a template left to replace, unclosed quote, single quotes, <code>></code> or <code>|</code>, unknown variable, <code>%EXPORT%</code> used before the export;
** wrong paths: a full path such as <code>%PROJECT%</code> placed after a folder (use <code>%PROJECTNAME%</code>), a <code>C:/…</code> path given to <code>cmd</code> (write <code>C:\…</code>), a folder that does not exist.
* '''Test''' runs the command right away (after confirmation) and tells whether it succeeded. For <code>curl</code>, the usual errors are explained (device not found, not reachable, error answered, no answer in time).
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Rules to know:
* Put an argument containing spaces between double quotes (<code>"%EXPORT%.3mf"</code>).
* Commands are run directly, without a shell: commands built into the Windows command prompt (<code>copy</code>, <code>del</code>, <code>echo</code>…) need <code>cmd /c</code> in front of them, and <code>></code>, <code>|</code> or <code>&&</code> do not work.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
=== Before or after the export ===
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
* '''After the export''' (default): the commands run in order once the 3MF is written and the slicer launched, before the end message. FreeCAD is not blocked while a delay runs.
* '''Before the export''' checked: the command runs as soon as the options are validated, before the meshing. Typical use: turn on the printer and start preheating while FreeCAD exports. The macro waits for each delay (countdown in the status bar) and the export only starts after the last "before" command. The 3MF is not written yet at that time: do not use <code>%EXPORT%</code> in these commands.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Example: a "before" command turns on the Shelly plug, a second "before" command with a 10 s delay sends the preheating to Klipper once the printer has started, then the export begins.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
The "before" commands are stored separately in the .ini file (EXTRA_COMMANDS_BEFORE): an older version of the macro ignores them.
</div>

<span id="Using_%PROJECT%,_%PROJECTDIR%,_%PROJECTNAME%_%EXPORT%,_and_%EXPORTNAME%_in_user_commands"></span>
=== Utilisation de %PROJECT%, %PROJECTDIR%, %PROJECTNAME% %EXPORT% et %EXPORTNAME% dans les commandes utilisateur ===

<div class="mw-translate-fuzzy">
Lorsque vous définissez des commandes de post-traitement personnalisées dans le flux de travail, vous pouvez utiliser trois espaces réservés spéciaux. Ces espaces réservés sont automatiquement remplacés par des valeurs dérivées de votre fichier de projet FreeCAD.
</div>

<span id="Available_placeholders"></span>
== Emplacements disponibles ==

<div class="mw-translate-fuzzy">
Emplacements et leur signification
* %PROJECT% : chemin d'accès complet du projet FreeCAD sans extension
* %PROJECTDIR% : dossier contenant le projet FreeCAD
* %PROJECTNAME% : nom du fichier de projet sans extension
*%EXPORT% : chemin d'accès complet de l'objet sélectionné à exporter, sans extension
*%EXPORTNAME% : nom du fichier d'exportation, sans extension
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
To build a path in another folder, use the names: <code>"C:\Backup\%EXPORTNAME%.3mf"</code>, not <code>"C:\Backup\%EXPORT%.3mf"</code>.
</div>

